1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,943
Hello,

2
00:00:00,943 --> 00:00:05,979
and thank you for asking me to your teachers' meeting to talk about the Dinosaur Museum

3
00:00:05,979 --> 00:00:09,209
and to tell you a bit about what you can do with your students

4
00:00:09,209 --> 00:00:09,490
there.

5
00:00:09,490 --> 00:00:13,800
Well, let me give you some of the basic information first.

6
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:19,596
In regard to opening hours, we're open every day of the week from 9.00

7
00:00:19,596 --> 00:00:24,308
am to 8.00 pm except on Mondays when we close at 1.30

8
00:00:24,308 --> 00:00:24,670
pm.

9
00:00:24,670 --> 00:00:28,412
And, in fact the only day in the year when we're closed is on

10
00:00:28,412 --> 00:00:29,800
the 25th of December.

11
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:34,628
You can book a guided tour for your school group any time that we're

12
00:00:34,628 --> 00:00:35,030
open.

13
00:00:35,030 --> 00:00:38,343
If you bring a school group to the museum,

14
00:00:38,343 --> 00:00:42,419
when you arrive we ask you to remain with your group in the car

15
00:00:42,419 --> 00:00:42,760
park.

16
00:00:42,760 --> 00:00:46,342
One or more of the tour guides will welcome you there

17
00:00:46,342 --> 00:00:49,260
and brief you about what the tour will be about.

18
00:00:49,260 --> 00:00:53,475
We do this there because our entrance is quite small

19
00:00:53,475 --> 00:00:57,970
and we really haven't got much room for briefing groups in the exhibition area.

20
00:00:57,970 --> 00:01:03,471
As far as the amount of time you'll need goes, if you bring a

21
00:01:03,471 --> 00:01:08,089
school group you should plan on allowing a minimum of 90 minutes for the

22
00:01:08,089 --> 00:01:08,470
visit.

23
00:01:08,470 --> 00:01:15,277
This allows 15 minutes to get on and off the coach, 45 minutes for the guided tour

24
00:01:15,277 --> 00:01:18,690
and 30 minutes for after-tour activities.

25
00:01:18,690 --> 00:01:23,674
If you're going to have lunch at the museum you will, of course, have

26
00:01:23,674 --> 00:01:24,860
to allow more time.

27
00:01:24,860 --> 00:01:29,800
There are two cafes in the museum, with seating for 80 people.

28
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:34,761
If you want to eat there you'll need to reserve some seating, as they

29
00:01:34,761 --> 00:01:36,810
can get quite crowded at lunch time.

30
00:01:36,810 --> 00:01:40,805
Then outside the museum at the back there are tables,

31
00:01:40,805 --> 00:01:44,479
and students can bring their own lunch and eat it there in the open

32
00:01:44,479 --> 00:01:44,740
air.

33
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:50,294
When the students come into the museum foyer we ask them to check in

34
00:01:50,294 --> 00:01:55,487
their backpacks with their books, lunch boxes, etc, at the cloakroom before they enter

35
00:01:55,487 --> 00:01:56,490
the museum proper.

36
00:01:56,490 --> 00:02:01,632
I'm afraid in the past we have had a few things gone missing after school visits

37
00:02:01,632 --> 00:02:03,640
so this is a strict rule.

38
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:08,712
Also, some of the exhibits are fragile and we don't want them to be

39
00:02:08,712 --> 00:02:10,000
accidentally knocked.

40
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,430
But we do provide school students with handouts with questions and quizzes on them.

41
00:02:15,430 --> 00:02:19,114
There's so much that students can learn in the museum

42
00:02:19,114 --> 00:02:21,650
and it's fun for them to have something to do.

43
00:02:21,650 --> 00:02:25,600
Of course they'll need to bring something to write with for these.

44
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:29,200
We do allow students to take photographs.

45
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:33,998
For students who are doing projects it's useful to make some kind of visual

46
00:02:33,998 --> 00:02:37,330
record of what they see that they can add to their reports.

47
00:02:37,330 --> 00:02:42,633
And finally, they should not bring anything to eat into the museum,

48
00:02:42,633 --> 00:02:44,360
or drinks of any kind.

49
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:49,400
There are also a few things the students can do after the tour.

50
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:56,149
In the theatrette on the ground floor there are continuous screenings of short documentaries

51
00:02:56,149 --> 00:02:58,600
about dinosaurs which they can see at any time.

52
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:04,850
We used to have an activity room with more interactive things like making models of dinosaurs

53
00:03:04,850 --> 00:03:09,357
and drawing and painting pictures, even hunting for dinosaur eggs,

54
00:03:09,357 --> 00:03:14,886
but unfortunately the room was damaged in a bad storm recently when water came

55
00:03:14,886 --> 00:03:15,467
in the roof,

56
00:03:15,467 --> 00:03:17,630
so that's closed at the moment.

57
00:03:17,630 --> 00:03:22,992
But we do have an IT centre where students have access to CD ROMs

58
00:03:22,992 --> 00:03:25,060
with a range of dinosaur games.

59
00:03:25,060 --> 00:03:27,609
These games are a lot of fun,

60
00:03:27,609 --> 00:03:32,445
but they also teach the students about the lives of dinosaurs, how they found

61
00:03:32,445 --> 00:03:36,017
food, protected their habitat, survived threats,

62
00:03:36,017 --> 00:03:37,040
that kind of thing.

63
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:38,960
And...

64
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:40,880
I think that's all I have to tell you.

65
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:45,696
Please feel free to ask any questions if you would like to know anything

66
00:03:45,696 --> 00:03:46,040
else...
